{0 console-eio}

Progress rows for OCaml command-line tools, run with
{{:https://github.com/ocaml-multicore/eio}Eio}.

[console-eio] shows the tasks of a program ({!Console.Task}) as progress
rows on the terminal. On a pipe or in a CI log it prints plain lines
instead. The smallest program:

{@ocaml[
let build root =
  Console.Task.run root "compile" (fun step ->
      Console.Task.set_items step ~count:1 ~total:2;
      Console.Task.log step "ocamlopt parser.ml";
      Console.Task.set_items step ~count:2 ~total:2)

let main () =
  Eio_main.run @@ fun env ->
  Console_eio.run ~clock:(Eio.Stdenv.mono_clock env) ~label:"Building" build
]}

The program's entry point is then [let () = main ()].

The {!page-tutorial} builds a whole command step by step, with options for
colour and theme, a table, progress rows and a test of its output.

{1:run What [Console_eio.run] does}

{!Console_eio.run} makes the root of the work and passes it to the
function, finds out whether the output is a terminal, and how large it is,
and redraws the progress rows ten times a second. It hands
each line the program prints, and each
{{:https://erratique.ch/software/logs}Logs} message, to the task current
where it is printed, or prints it above the rows outside any task
({{!Console_eio.output}where text goes}).

The terminal is given back as [run] found it when the function returns or
raises, when its fiber is cancelled, and when the program exits or is
stopped by Ctrl-C or SIGTERM.

{1:rules Running tasks}

{ul
{- The work succeeds when the function returns and fails when it raises,
   unless the function ended the root itself, with a message, and the
   tasks still running end with it. An exception the function raises still
   reaches the caller.}
{- A {!Console_eio.run} called inside another one, or in a fiber beside
   it, gets the task current where it is called, or the root, so a library
   can start one without knowing whether its caller did. The display
   closes once the last of them ends.}
{- Call the functions of tasks only from the domain that runs them. A [Logs]
   message logged in another domain that Eio runs, such as one of an
   [Eio.Executor_pool], is passed to that domain and printed above the
   rows.}
{- What [print_string], [Printf] or a child process writes on the terminal
   goes past console and garbles the rows, so print it through
   {!Console_eio.with_output}.}
{- {!Console_eio.Prompt} asks a question, or a password without echoing it,
   and takes the rows off the screen while it asks.}}

{1:modules Modules}

{ul
{- {!Console_eio}: runs a display and decides on colour for the outputs the
   program left alone.}
{- {!Console_eio.Prompt}: questions asked on the terminal, a line, a yes or
   no, or a password.}}

The widgets, the themes and the rules of the display are in the [console]
package: {!Console}.
